  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the points of intersection between the rational function \\( f(x) \\) and the function \\( g(x) \\), and then compute the expression \\( \\frac{y_2}{y_1} + \\frac{x_2}{x_1} \\).\n\nFirst, let's find the points of intersection. We set \\( f(x) = g(x) \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2x + \\sqrt{3}}{x - \\sqrt{3}} = x + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{x}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate the fractions, we multiply both sides by \\( x(x - \\sqrt{3}) \\):\n\n\\[\n(2x + \\sqrt{3})x = x(x - \\sqrt{3})\\left(x + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{x}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n(2x + \\sqrt{3})x = x(x^2 - \\sqrt{3}x + \\sqrt{3})\n\\]\n\nDistribute \\( x \\) on both sides:\n\n\\[\n2x^2 + \\sqrt{3}x = x^3 - \\sqrt{3}x^2 + \\sqrt{3}x\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\( 2x^2 + \\sqrt{3}x \\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n0 = x^3 - \\sqrt{3}x^2\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\( x^2 \\):\n\n\\[\n0 = x^2(x - \\sqrt{3})\n\\]\n\nSo, the solutions are \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( x = \\sqrt{3} \\).\n\nNow, we find the corresponding \\( y \\)-values for these \\( x \\)-values.\n\nFor \\( x = 0 \\):\n\n\\[\ny = g(0) = 0 + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{0} \\quad \\text{(undefined)}\n\\]\n\nFor \\( x = \\sqrt{3} \\):\n\n\\[\ny = g(\\sqrt{3}) = \\sqrt{3} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\sqrt{3} + 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the points of intersection are \\( (0, \\text{undefined}) \\) and \\( (\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3} + 1) \\).\n\nHowever, since the function \\( g(x) \\) is undefined at \\( x = 0 \\), we discard \\( (0, \\text{undefined}) \\) and consider only \\( (\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3} + 1) \\).\n\nNow, we compute \\( \\frac{y_2}{y_1} + \\frac{x_2}{x_1} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_2}{y_1} + \\frac{x_2}{x_1} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} + 1}{1} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} = (\\sqrt{3} + 1) + 1 = \\sqrt{3} + 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{3} + 2}\n\\]",
